WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.660
The revival of NBC's "Night Court" became an&nbsp;
instant hit in early 2023, thanks in large part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.220
to the return of a fan-favorite character. But&nbsp;
this is not the same Dan Fielding. Here's why.

00:00:11.401 --> 00:00:15.721
Much of the revival's appeal comes from a&nbsp;
carefully considered balance of nostalgia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.721 --> 00:00:20.700
and likable new elements. Credit for that&nbsp;
goes directly to series star Melissa Rauch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.940
who got the revival going as part of&nbsp;
her deal with Warner Bros. Television.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.540
There was one link to the previous&nbsp;
series that couldn't be overlooked:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.760
John Larroquette. His performance as assistant&nbsp;
district attorney Dan Fielding garnered four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.560
consecutive Emmy awards for supporting actor&nbsp;
in a comedy series. Bringing Fielding back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.855
meant dealing with a familiar character's&nbsp;
past, present, and future all at once.

00:00:41.855 --> 00:00:44.100
Dan Fielding has been described as many things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.320
but in a nutshell he's a lothario who cares&nbsp;
only about his own desires. "Night Court"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.807
viewers in the '80s and '90s were often treated&nbsp;
to Fielding's exploits with a host of women.

00:00:53.807 --> 00:00:57.780
While some were humorous, others hinted&nbsp;
at a sense of sadness and longing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.200
assistant district attorney hid&nbsp;
all too well. Reintroducing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.500
character after thirty years offered&nbsp;
an opportunity to delve deeper into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.640
who he has become and avoid treading&nbsp;
water with regurgitated plot points.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.960
The "Night Court" revival addressed where&nbsp;
Fielding landed in its first episode. Judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.640
Abby Stone sought out the character after losing&nbsp;
a key component to her court. Instead of offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.563
him his old job, the new judge asks him&nbsp;
to assume the role of public defender.

00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:26.400
The first exchange between Stone, daughter of&nbsp;
former judge Harry Stone, and Fielding quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.380
sets the tone for where he stands today. Gone is&nbsp;
the ladies' man who was constantly pursuing new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.780
partners, and what remains is a man changed&nbsp;
by life's biggest moments — love and loss.

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:39.299
"That is what sold Sarah on the place."

00:01:39.575 --> 00:01:40.802
"Is Sarah your wife?" 

00:01:41.556 --> 00:01:42.180
"She was."

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.840
The former assistant district attorney&nbsp;
revealed that he had indeed been married,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.460
finding love with his deceased wife. Her loss&nbsp;
left Fielding searching for some solace but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.420
only finding work as a process server.&nbsp;
The job kept him near the legal community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.260
without practicing law — something he&nbsp;
grew tired of while viewers were away.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.520
Much like in the original series, there's&nbsp;
more than meets the eye with Fielding. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.180
Fielding's return also came a new,&nbsp;
more modern attitude towards women.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.960
No longer was he the man who couldn't&nbsp;
encounter a female character without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.400
making salacious comments. He's still&nbsp;
sardonic, but he’s clearly much wiser.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.079
Early episodes touch on Dan Fielding's current&nbsp;
thoughts on dating and love as a widower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.079 --> 00:02:24.240
with Episode 4, "Dan vs. Dating," offering&nbsp;
the clearest picture of where he is now. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.780
the episode, a woman asks him for his&nbsp;
phone number, but he initially avoids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.220
getting in touch with her, choosing instead&nbsp;
to dine alone. Abby's clerk, Neil, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.779
pushes "Send" on Dan's drafted text, and&nbsp;
the woman later meets him at the restaurant.

00:02:36.779 --> 00:02:40.500
The latter part of the episode is where&nbsp;
the audience experiences a deep dive into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.280
someone they thought they knew. It turns&nbsp;
out that the woman just wanted a "fling,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:50.220
which was actually a cover for an identity theft&nbsp;
revenge plot related to an old case, so Fielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.360
unwittingly discovers how much his wife's&nbsp;
memory lingers. Before the ruse is revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.740
what would have previously been a one-night stand&nbsp;
becomes a strong source of character development.

00:02:58.931 --> 00:03:03.311
"She changed me. I mean, the old me&nbsp;
would have been very much into this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.311 --> 00:03:05.820
but I'm a different man now."

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.400
TV Fanatic's Diana Keng encapsulated what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.640
many viewers were feeling after&nbsp;
the episode's debut, writing,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.060
"There is sweeping romanticism in&nbsp;
the thought of Dan Fielding giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.620
up his rapscallion ways for the love of a good&nbsp;
woman. Or maybe the love of the right woman."

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.060
None of this would be possible without the&nbsp;
talents of John Larroquette. Long against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.060
returning to the role, the process&nbsp;
of meeting Rauch and seeing what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.080
the creative team had in mind convinced&nbsp;
him to give it a try. Producing as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.200
as starring in the series gave him the&nbsp;
ability to shape his character's current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.940
incarnation — something expertly acted in&nbsp;
this episode as well as the entire revival.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.620
According to the actor, don't expect Fielding&nbsp;
to be back to his old ways. He told Deadline,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.468
"The man is 75 years old, nobody wants to see&nbsp;
that and I certainly don't want to play that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.468 --> 00:03:54.748
So whatever we'll do in the future about that will&nbsp;
always be glancing blows to that sort of homage."

